Body:
This use case is already supported by the community plugin:

https://github.com/yuhp/opencode-models-discovery

The plugin discovers models from `/v1/models` for configured `@ai-sdk/openai-compatible` providers and injects them into `provider.<name>.models` during the config hook.

It supports provider-specific endpoints, timeouts, model filtering, OpenCode `/connect` credentials, optional persisted caching, and provider-specific metadata enrichment. Explicitly configured models are preserved and take precedence over discovered values.

Example:

```json
{
  "plugin": ["opencode-models-discovery@latest"],
  "provider": {
    "my-provider": {
      "npm": "@ai-sdk/openai-compatible",
      "name": "My Provider",
      "options": {
        "baseURL": "http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1",
        "modelsDiscovery": {
          "enabled": true
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

This LM Studio use case is supported by the community plugin:

https://github.com/yuhp/opencode-models-discovery

The plugin queries the actual LM Studio `/v1/models` endpoint instead of relying only on the static models.dev catalog, so the discovered model list reflects the models exposed by the local server.

It can also use LM Studio's `/api/v1/models` inventory endpoint for metadata enrichment:

```json
{
  "plugin": ["opencode-models-discovery@latest"],
  "provider": {
    "lmstudio": {
      "npm": "@ai-sdk/openai-compatible",
      "name": "LM Studio",
      "options": {
        "baseURL": "http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1",
        "modelsDiscovery": {
          "enabled": true,
          "modelInfoFormat": "lmstudio"
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Workaround for anyone who'd rather not wait on a fix: the clamp can be overridden from user config, no patch and no plugin needed.

```jsonc
// ~/.config/opencode/opencode.json
"provider": {
  "openai": {
    "models": {
      "gpt-5.5":            { "limit": { "context": 1050000, "input": 922000, "output": 128000 } },
      "gpt-5.5-fast":       { "limit": { "context": 1050000, "input": 922000, "output": 128000 } },
      "gpt-5.6-sol":        { "limit": { "context": 1050000, "input": 922000, "output": 128000 } },
      "gpt-5.6-sol-fast":   { "limit": { "context": 1050000, "input": 922000, "output": 128000 } },
      "gpt-5.6-terra":      { "limit": { "context": 1050000, "input": 922000, "output": 128000 } },
      "gpt-5.6-terra-fast": { "limit": { "context": 1050000, "input": 922000, "output": 128000 } },
      "gpt-5.6-luna":       { "limit": { "context": 1050000, "input": 922000, "output": 128000 } },
      "gpt-5.6-luna-fast":  { "limit": { "context": 1050000, "input": 922000, "output": 128000 } }
    }
  }
}
```

Those are the untouched models.dev registry values, and `1050000 = 922000 + 128000`, so `context` is input plus output allowance.

It works because user config is applied after the plugin.

Verified on 1.18.30 by diffing `GET /provider` between two isolated servers (separate `OPENCODE_CONFIG` and `OPENCODE_DB`). Before, all eight of `gpt-5.5`, `gpt-5.5-fast`, `gpt-5.6-{sol,terra,luna}` and their `-fast` variants report `context 400000 / input 272000`. After, all eight report `1050000 / 922000`, and the full model-object diff shows `limit` as the only changed field: the plugin's zeroed cost, the `serviceTier` option on the `-fast` variants and all six reasoning variants survive untouched. Confirmed in a real session too, where the context readout for 21,358 tokens went from 5% to 2%.

Two caveats. The schema at [v1/config/provider.ts#L47-L53](https://github.com/anomalyco/opencode/blob/193de13a88d62a6409c6d385831180f1def527dc/packages/core/src/v1/config/provider.ts#L47-L53) makes `context` and `output` required inside `limit`, so all three fields have to be present even though only `input` drives compaction. And the registry prices these models at a 2x tier above 272,000 context, which costs nothing on ChatGPT sign-in, but I have no evidence either way about how plan quota accounting treats that range, so I wouldn't assume the extra context is free in quota terms.

This is a workaround, not a substitute for the fix: it needs every user to know the registry values and to re-add entries whenever a new Codex model gets clamped. It also doesn't cover the extra v2 problem in #47646, where `gpt-6-astra` and `gpt-5.4` get clamped too, and that config path would need checking separately.
